NAME
    srm - System Repair & Maintenance: diagnose, verify and repair TOS

SYNOPSIS
    srm
    srm status
    srm scan
    srm health
    srm verify
    srm full
    srm baseline [--full]
    srm repair   [--restore] [--source <mount>] [--unverified]
    srm restore  [--source <mount>] [--unverified] [path...]

DESCRIPTION
    srm is the front door to every maintenance tool in TOS. The individual
    commands it wraps still work on their own — 'doctor' for runtime health,
    'verify' for file integrity — but srm runs them together, adds the two
    things neither could do alone, and reports everything with one severity
    scale.

    SRM HAS TWO HALVES.

    SRM Basic lives in the EEPROM, inside the BIOS. It runs at POST and
    catches the faults that stop a boot before anything on disk can run: a
    CPU that can't execute the kernel, no boot device, a kernel that isn't
    there, an /init.lua that won't load. When it hits one it names the fault
    on screen, beeps it (one long tone, then a number of short ones equal to
    the code's digit — so a machine with a dead screen is still diagnosable),
    and PARKS the code in the EEPROM. That is the only storage that survives
    a machine whose disk is the problem.

    SRM Advanced is this command. It has a disk and megabytes of room, so it
    covers everything the EEPROM has no space for: memory headroom, disk
    usage, services, accounts, the log, security posture, every file in the
    manifest, and drift from a known-good baseline. On the first successful
    boot after a POST failure it reads the parked code, explains it in
    English, and clears it — so a fault that killed a boot last night is
    still waiting to be explained this morning.

    FAULT CODES (parked by SRM Basic)
      C1  CPU architecture too old — TOS needs a Lua 5.3+ CPU
      D2  no boot device — no /init.lua disk and no TBFS drive found
      B3  the TBFS boot blob would not compile
      K4  the kernel was missing (/tos/kernel/init.lua)
      I5  /init.lua could not be opened
      I6  /init.lua would not compile

    THE KNOWN-GOOD BASELINE
    'verify' compares files against the manifest, which says what SHOULD be
    installed. It cannot tell you a file was quietly changed. 'srm baseline'
    records a SHA-256 of every boot-critical file as it stands right now, and
    'srm scan' reports anything that has since gone missing or changed.

    With --full it also stores a verified copy of each file under
    /var/srm/store, which makes local repair possible: 'srm repair --restore'
    puts the damaged file back. Without --full only hashes are kept (a few
    hundred bytes instead of a couple of hundred KB), drift is still detected,
    and repair needs an external source — an install floppy, say — passed
    with --source.

    A restore NEVER writes a copy it has not checked. Both the local store and
    an external source must hash-match the baseline, or the file is refused
    and left alone. Without a baseline there is nothing to check against, so
    the operation refuses unless you accept that explicitly with
    --unverified. Restores are written atomically: the target is replaced by
    rename, never truncated, so a failure mid-repair cannot leave a
    zero-length system file.

    Capturing a baseline is a claim about what "good" means on this machine.
    Take one on a system you trust — a fresh install, or one that has just
    passed 'srm verify'. A baseline taken on a damaged system certifies the
    damage.

SUBCOMMANDS
    status      What SRM knows right now: any parked POST fault, whether the
                last shutdown was clean, whether a baseline exists and what
                the store costs. Hashes nothing, so it is instant. (Default.)
    scan        Hash every baselined file and report drift, deletion, and
                store rot.
    health      Runtime health. Identical to 'doctor'.
    verify      File integrity against the manifest. Identical to 'verify'.
    full        status, health, verify and scan in one report.
    baseline    Record the current system as known-good. --full also stores
                verified copies for local repair.
    repair      Run the fixer pass: finish interrupted writes, sweep orphaned
                temp files, rewrite a corrupt boot.cfg, clear stale runtime
                state, trim oversized logs. With --restore, also put back
                files that are missing or have drifted.
    restore     Restore specific files (or everything scan flags) from the
                store or from --source.

    status, scan, health, verify and full are open to any logged-in user.
    baseline, repair and restore require an ADMIN session.

FILES
    /var/srm/index.dat    the baseline: a hash and size per file
    /var/srm/store/...    verified copies, mirroring their real paths
    /etc/critical.bak     the boot-critical file LIST (written by pkg)

EXAMPLES
    srm                          Did anything go wrong? Instant answer.
    srm baseline --full          Record this system as good, with copies.
                                 Do this right after a clean install.
    srm scan                     Has anything changed since then?
    srm repair                   Fix what is mechanically safe to fix.
    srm repair --restore         ...and put drifted system files back.
    srm restore --source /mnt/fd0 /tos/kernel/fs.lua
                                 Restore one file from the install floppy,
                                 checked against the baseline first.
    srm full                     Everything, for a bug report.

NOTES
    Some repairs only work at boot, before the files they fix are read.
    'bootsettings repair on' schedules the fixer pass for the next boot; it
    runs once and clears its own flag, so a repair that crashes can never
    loop. That boot-time pass is this same code — the report is identical.

    The boot-time pass never restores files. Overwriting a system file is a
    decision an operator makes, not a fix that is safe to apply unattended.

